            "Claim: the availability argument is partly right and structurally incomplete, because availability is only the first of four stages and hunger in India is produced mainly at the access and absorption stages. Named evidence and example: the statutory entitlement of five kilograms per person per month for priority households and thirty-five kilograms per household per month for Antyodaya Anna Yojana households under the National Food Security Act, 2013, with the Department of Food and Public Distribution portal read on 2 September 2026 recording enactment on 5 July 2013, coverage of up to seventy-five per cent of the rural and fifty per cent of the urban population and a maximum coverage of 81.34 crore persons; the Act's life-cycle provisions and the maternity benefit of not less than six thousand rupees; the distinction between nutrition-specific and nutrition-sensitive interventions; and the National Family Health Survey record of under-five stunting at 35.5 per cent in the fifth round of 2019-21 and 29.3 per cent in the sixth round of 2023-24 released on 29 May 2026. Analysis: an availability-first reading explains procurement and buffer policy but cannot explain why a household holding a valid card remains undernourished, since access fails through identification, documentation and migration barriers, and absorption fails through diarrhoeal disease, unsafe water and poor sanitation, which is precisely why the reduction in stunting between two named rounds cannot be credited to grain allocation alone. Qualification: the human-development reading must not be pushed into denying that availability matters, because a chronic supply failure would make every downstream policy irrelevant, and each indicator must be carried with its survey round. Why this earns marks: it takes a position on a how-far-do-you-agree directive, uses the four-stage frame as the organising device and closes with a bounded verdict.",
