            "Claim: poverty and hunger diverge because they are measured by different instruments and repaired by different expenditures, so a fall in one is not evidence of a fall in the other. Named evidence and example: the owner's rule that income or calorie poverty, multidimensional poverty and anthropometry measure different deprivations; the four-stage availability, access, absorption and utilisation frame; the anganwadi service layer under Poshan 2.0 with the Ministry of Women and Child Development and the school layer under PM POSHAN with the Ministry of Education; and the first thousand days from conception to age two as the window in which deprivation becomes irreversible. Analysis: monetary poverty responds to income, transfer and employment expenditure, whereas hunger and undernutrition respond to service expenditure on anganwadi staffing, growth monitoring, therapeutic care, water and sanitation, so compressing the service head while transfers hold produces exactly the observed divergence, and because the critical window is short, a service compression today is recorded in the anthropometry of the next decade. Qualification: no expenditure figure or trend is asserted here, since budget numbers must be cited with their year and head, and the divergence claim itself must be carried with the survey rounds compared. Why this earns marks: it elucidates a mechanism connecting two measurements to two expenditure heads instead of describing poverty in general.",
