            "Claim: the cycle is intergenerational, so it is broken only at the points where deprivation becomes irreversible and where entitlement fails to travel with the person. Named evidence and example: the loop from income and asset deprivation through calorie and micronutrient deficit to impaired cognitive, physical and immune development, low human capital and low earnings; the first thousand days from conception to age two; Poshan 2.0 and the anganwadi layer under the Ministry of Women and Child Development; PM POSHAN in Classes I to VIII under the Ministry of Education; One Nation One Ration Card portability using Aadhaar-seeded cards and electronic point-of-sale machines; and the national anaemia strategy with its six-by-six-by-six design for adolescent girls and pregnant and lactating women. Analysis: the first thousand days fix the sequencing, because a stunted child becomes a lower-earning adult whose child is again undernourished, so maternal and adolescent nutrition are child-nutrition instruments rather than separate heads; portability addresses the exclusion produced by migration; and convergence with water, sanitation and hygiene addresses absorption, which no additional grain allocation can reach. Qualification: acute malnutrition is not answered by extra grain, since severe acute malnutrition at weight-for-height below minus three standard deviations requires facility-based therapeutic care, and every outcome cited must carry its survey round, with under-five stunting recorded at 35.5 per cent in the fifth round of 2019-21 and 29.3 per cent in the sixth round of 2023-24. Why this earns marks: it explains a loop, attaches each lever to the exact link it cuts and ends with a treatment and data qualification.",
